From 7131ac8fc9d8582100411174b8d76b3f253b4606 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Curd Becker <me@curd-becker.de>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:53:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] input: add a polled HID-over-I2C keyboard driver

Keyboards on HID over I2C (compatible "hid-over-i2c"), such as the
Microsoft Surface RT Type Cover, have no driver in U-Boot. HID over I2C
has no boot protocol, so the input report layout must come from the
device's report descriptor; the Type Cover's keyboard report, for
example, has ten key slots and no reserved byte after the modifiers.

hid_kbd gains hid_kbd_parse_layout(), which walks the report descriptor
for the keyboard application collection and takes from it the report ID,
the 8-bit modifier field (usages E0-E7) and the array of 8-bit key
usages, both byte-aligned. That is the shape of boot-like keyboards and
of the Type Cover, and keeps the parser to the items it needs (usage
page, usages, report ID, size and count, push/pop, collections, input).
Porting Linux hid-core's parser was rejected as far beyond what a
console keyboard needs. Key events come from hid_kbd_update(); reports
in a rollover or error state keep the previous keys.

The device protocol follows Linux i2c-hid: the HID descriptor is read
from "hid-descr-addr"; initialisation is SET_POWER ON (retried once after
500 us), RESET, up to 1 s for the zero-length reset response, SET_POWER
ON again; input is a plain read of wMaxInputLength bytes. The driver
enables "vdd-supply" and "vddl-supply" and waits
"post-power-on-delay-ms".

U-Boot polls, so the interrupt line is read as a GPIO, taken from the
node's "interrupts" with its interrupt parent as the GPIO controller
(two cells, the first the GPIO number, as on Tegra); input is read only
while it is asserted.

The device may be absent at probe and may be detached and re-attached at
any time: the stdio device is registered at probe without touching the
device, and the device is initialised on the first poll that reaches it.
A transfer error releases all held keys and marks the device detached;
initialisation is retried every 100 ms. While attached and idle, a
4-byte read of the HID descriptor every 100 ms checks the device is still
there, because a detached device's interrupt line stays inactive and no
input read would notice. A device whose descriptor has no supported
keyboard is left alone until it is detached. A detach and re-attach
while nothing polls the console (during a long-running command) is not
seen; the device then stays uninitialised.

With ACPIGEN the generic I2C chip driver binds the same compatible, so
the driver depends on !ACPIGEN.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
